      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet. The dose looks like it may be an error.\n\n**Metformin 1700 mg PO TID = 5100 mg/day**, which is about double the labeled maximum for immediate-release metformin (2550 mg/day). Metformin is also not commonly available as 1700 mg, and standard tablet strengths are 500, 850, and 1000 mg.\n\nSome possibilities:\n- **850 mg PO TID with meals** (2550 mg/day, the labeled maximum for IR)\n- **1000 mg PO BID with meals** (2000 mg/day)\n- **1700 mg PO BID** (3400 mg/day), if you intended a different total, though this is still above the usual IR maximum\n- **Extended-release** formulation, which is dosed once daily with a labeled maximum of 2000 mg/day\n\nPlease confirm the intended dose, formulation, and frequency, and I'll enter the order. The patient's normal renal and hepatic function is good, but a 5100 mg/day dose would still exceed the labeled maximum and raise the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity."
      }
     ],
